WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
A white dwarf is a small, hot carbon

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.460
and oxygen remnant that cools after ordinary core

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.940
fusion ends. Electron degeneracy supplies pressure: crowding forces

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.460
electrons into higher momentum states because identical electrons

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.520
cannot all share the same quantum state. This

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.780
is not a claim that matter becomes literally

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:19.900
incompressible.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.020
The limiting white dwarf mass is about one

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.600
point four times the Sun’s mass. This limit

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.020
applies to a remnant supported by electron degeneracy.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.260
It does not describe the original star’s birth

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.560
mass. A progenitor can lose substantial material before

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.220
leaving its core. Neutron stars have a separate

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.460
support problem with a different mass limit.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.910
On a conventional H R diagram, white dwarfs

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:45.990
lie in the hot, low luminosity region. A

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.410
small surface area can outweigh a high surface

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.830
temperature. At equal temperature, reducing radius to one

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:55.350
tenth reduces luminosity to one hundredth. A hot

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.910
surface therefore does not guarantee a large total

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:58.770
light output.
